**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 1 in full; [Química Geral I — Feitosa, Barbosa, and Forte](../sources/Quimica-Geral-I-UECE.lingua#r_7N9JXVMC5DWB8P3MX79YYPHNPA), chapter 1 in full; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), What is Chemistry all about? completely.

Chemistry asks what a material contains, how its constituents are arranged, how they interact, and how a change can be explained or controlled. Start with an ordinary question: why can a clear liquid leave a solid after evaporation, and what evidence would identify that solid? Appearance alone cannot establish composition. A transparent liquid can be a pure substance or a mixture of many species.

Separate a question about identity, a question about quantity, a question about structure, and a question about transformation. “There is copper in this specimen” differs from a mass fraction, an oxidation state, or a claim about how the copper will react. A useful investigation states the specimen, conditions, observation, candidate explanations, and what further evidence would distinguish them.

Choose a material boundary as well as a conceptual one. A reaction vessel can exchange matter and energy with its surroundings; an unexplained loss may be evaporation, a gas product, a transfer loss, or an inadequate measurement. Chemistry connects small-scale species with the larger flows studied in extraction, water treatment, manufacturing, medicine, and maintenance.

**Work:** describe table salt, a metal object, water, and a rock without identifying their composition from appearance alone. For each write an observable property, a possible chemical explanation, and a test or reference that would change the explanation. Use a drawing or existing observations; the first exercise does not require handling unknown substances.

Read the opening chapters completely and keep returning to the distinction between evidence and representation. Later calculations are useful only when their symbols refer to a stated chemical system. [Philosophy](../../../../../scholazein/philosophy/Philosophy.lingua#r_00000000000000000000001001) and [Inquiry — shared ways to understand and investigate](../../../inquiry/Inquiry.lingua#r_75MNKZQKNB0XBG021SWWW2HASB) provide a return for questions about classification, models, and what an observation justifies.

**Continue:** [Substances, mixtures, components, and phases](Substances-mixtures-and-phases.lingua#r_07Y9PYVRA9HSVSZWQC4N0X8ZZS).

